Learning goals
By the end of this week, you should be able to:

transfer TRACE to unfamiliar information and AI problems;
identify which TRACE step a workflow is missing;
choose an appropriate verification standard from context;
distinguish confidence from evidence quality;
audit your Final Project for missing provenance, probes, verification, and judgment;
state a personal rule for when to use, modify, disclose, reject, or stop AI use.

Session 2 — Missing-step audit
Each group receives a flawed workflow.
Examples:

strong prompt, no verification;
many sources, no provenance;
verified facts, inflated causal claim;
clear AI disclosure, no independent work;
human approval, no real override authority;
huge context, no context control.

Identify the missing TRACE capacity.

TRACE transfer matrix
Ask whether the same method works across:

academic work;
workplace analysis;
public information;
high-stakes decisions;
everyday AI use.

The details change.
The epistemic discipline should remain recognisable.

Session 3 — Final Project readiness audit
Audit all three frozen tasks.

For every task confirm

provenance captured;
authentic task clearly defined;
AI role documented;
prompts/interactions preserved;
at least one meaningful probe;
independent verification planned;
failure/limitation evidence preserved;
final human judgment explicit;
AI-use disclosure complete.



Across all three tasks confirm

tasks are meaningfully different;
comparison criteria are common enough to compare;
verification cost can be discussed;
one genuine failure case is documented;
your Personal AI/LLM Use Charter is evidence-based.

Task 14 — TRACE Transfer Audit
Submit:

one unfamiliar case from today’s transfer activity;
the TRACE step that most changed your initial judgment;
one weakness you identified in your own routine;
how you will correct that weakness;
Final Project Task A readiness status;
Final Project Task B readiness status;
Final Project Task C readiness status;
any unresolved evidence or verification gap;
your strongest portfolio artifact and why;
your provisional one-sentence Personal AI/LLM Use Charter;
AI-use disclosure.

Submit Task 14 — TRACE Transfer Audit

Learning goals
By the end of this week, you should be able to:

distinguish a general risk-management framework from a profession-specific rule;
identify how stakes, reversibility, confidentiality, and duty of care change acceptable AI use;
define a verification threshold appropriate to a task;
identify when sensitive or confidential information should not be entered into an AI system;
construct context-specific AI-use guidelines;
submit three discipline-specific Final Project task candidates with risk and verification plans.

Task 12 — Final Project Gate 1
Submit three candidate Final Project tasks.
For each task provide:

task title;
discipline/professional context;
authentic purpose;
expected AI role;
main risks;
confidentiality/data concerns;
independent verification plan;
evidence you will preserve;
expected failure mode;
why this task adds something different from the other two.

Finish with:

proposed cross-task comparison question;
initial idea for your Personal AI/LLM Use Charter;
AI-use disclosure.

Submit Task 12 — Final Project Gate 1

Learning goals
By the end of this week, you should be able to:

identify several forms of hallucination or unsupported generation;
distinguish a plausible citation from a verified citation;
verify claims by opening and inspecting primary or authoritative sources;
recognise source distortion even when the cited source is real;
explain why confidence and fluency are not reliability measures;
construct a claim-by-claim verification dossier.

Session 2 — Verification dossier

Claim decomposition
Take one paragraph and break it into atomic claims.
For each claim record:

claim;
source supplied;
source exists?;
source supports claim?;
independent evidence?;
verdict.



Pair Challenge — Real source, wrong claim
Pairs receive a set containing:

a real and correctly used source;
a fabricated source;
a real source that does not support the claim;
a partly supported claim that is overstated.

The objective is to distinguish source existence from source adequacy.

Task 10 — Verification Dossier
Generate or select one AI-assisted answer containing at least five externally checkable claims.
Submit:

original question/task;
original AI output;
five claims extracted from the output;
supplied source/citation for each claim, if any;
existence check;
source-to-claim match check;
independent verification where appropriate;
verdict for each claim;
corrected answer;
one example of the most dangerous plausible error;
AI-use disclosure.

Submit Task 10 — Verification Dossier

Learning goals
By the end of this week, you should be able to:

distinguish prompt decoration from meaningful task specification;
specify task, context, constraints, output criteria, and evidence requirements;
improve a weak prompt through controlled iteration;
request intermediate products and concise rationale without asking for hidden chain-of-thought;
compare outputs using explicit quality criteria rather than preference alone;
identify which prompt improvements transfer across model providers.

Session 2 — Decomposition without hidden reasoning

Staged prompting
For a complex task, separate:

understand the task;
identify missing information;
produce a plan or intermediate artifact;
produce the requested output;
check the output against criteria.

You may ask for:

assumptions;
a short rationale;
a checklist;
a comparison table;
evidence to inspect;
uncertainty or limitations.

Do not depend on requests for private or hidden chain-of-thought.


Pair Challenge — Repair the prompt
Each pair receives a weak prompt from a different domain.
Produce:

Version 1: minimal repair;
Version 2: structured task specification;
Version 3: verification-aware prompt.

Compare usefulness, not verbosity.

Session 3 — Provider-independent prompting

Cross-provider principle audit
From the three preparation resources, identify principles that survive changes in provider terminology.
Candidate principles may include:

clarity;
context;
examples when needed;
constraints;
output specification;
iteration;
evaluation.



Decide
For your task, identify:

one instruction that clearly improved performance;
one instruction that added complexity without value;
one aspect that still required human judgment.



Verify
Check at least one factual or source-based output independently.

Task 08 — Prompt Reconstruction
Choose one authentic, low-risk task from your discipline.
Start with a deliberately weak prompt and improve it through three controlled versions.
Submit:

target task;
weak baseline prompt;
Version 1;
Version 2;
Version 3;
what changed between versions;
your output-quality criteria;
evidence showing which version performed better;
one instruction that did not help;
one independently verified element;
AI-use disclosure.

Submit Task 08 — Prompt Reconstruction

Learning goals
By the end of this week, you should be able to:

distinguish false positives and false negatives;
explain why “overall accuracy” can hide asymmetric errors;
explain why fairness is not one universal statistic;
describe how base rates can create tension between fairness metrics;
use controlled counterfactual changes to probe a decision system;
distinguish a legitimate decision criterion from a questionable proxy;
identify who bears responsibility for using an algorithmic score.

Session 3 — Who owns the decision?

Pair Challenge — Decision responsibility map
Choose one context:

hiring;
lending;
scholarship;
content moderation;
insurance;
public administration.

Map:

Data → Model → Score → Human interpretation → Decision → Consequence

For each stage identify possible bias, verification opportunity, and responsible actor.


Decide
Would you:

use the system as-is;
use it only as decision support;
require an audit;
remove a feature;
reject the system for this use?

Defend the decision.


Verify
What independent evidence would you need before approving this system for real-world use?

Task 06 — Counterfactual Fairness Probe
Use an instructor-approved synthetic decision scenario.
Do not submit sensitive personal data.
Submit:

original synthetic case;
original decision/output;
three one-variable-at-a-time counterfactual changes;
resulting decisions;
one variable you regard as substantively legitimate;
one variable that could operate as a proxy;
what your small test can establish;
what it cannot establish;
the fairness criterion you consider most important for this context and why;
AI-use disclosure.


Week 7 case seed
At the end of Task 06, register one candidate digital artifact or decision problem that you may investigate in the Week 7 TRACE Midterm Challenge.
The case seed is not graded for quality. It exists to establish provenance before the challenge begins.
Submit Task 06 — Counterfactual Fairness Probe

TRACE Midterm Challenge
Week 7 is not a conventional written exam.
Before class:

make sure your Week 6 case seed is submitted;
review your W1–W6 Tasks;
bring access to your evidence links/screenshots;
complete the readiness quiz.

No new reading is assigned.
Open Week 7 Readiness Quiz

Learning goals
By the end of this week, you should be able to:

explain next-token generation conceptually without equations;
explain why output depends on preceding context;
describe self-attention at an intuitive level;
distinguish model training, generation, and external retrieval/tool use;
explain why repeated runs can produce different outputs;
design a controlled comparison in which one interaction variable is changed at a time.

Task 04 — Model Variation Log
Choose one discipline-relevant, non-sensitive task.
Run it under at least three controlled conditions.
Submit:

target task;
baseline prompt;
Condition A/B/C;
exact variable changed;
outputs or evidence links;
stable features;
unstable features;
one externally verified element;
what the comparison does not allow you to conclude;
AI-use disclosure.

Submit Task 04 — Model Variation Log

Learning goals
By the end of this week, you should be able to:

distinguish ranking, recommendation, personalisation, and targeting;
explain why being shown content does not by itself prove that the content changed someone’s behaviour;
identify how query wording and platform design can alter the information environment;
distinguish evidence of data collection, profiling, targeting, exposure, and persuasion;
conduct a small visibility audit without overclaiming that differences prove manipulation;
explain why algorithmic influence claims require both mechanism evidence and outcome evidence.

Task 02 — Algorithmic Visibility Audit
Choose one non-sensitive information need relevant to your daily or academic life.
Run:

2 meaningfully different queries × 2 search/recommendation environments

Submit:

information need;
Query A and Query B;
System 1 and System 2;
top five results for each condition;
result overlap;
source-type differences;
one explanation for why visibility may have changed;
one explanation you cannot justify from your data;
a short distinction between visibility, targeting, and persuasion;
AI-use disclosure if applicable.

Submit Task 02 — Algorithmic Visibility Audit

TRACE Skill Portfolio — 30%
Weekly Tasks are primarily formative and receive:

COMPLETE
REVISE
MISSING

For the summative portfolio, select five strong artifacts from the course.
Together, the five selected artifacts must provide evidence for all seven evidence codes.

Portfolio submission must include

five selected artifacts;
an evidence index mapping each TRACE capacity to exact evidence;
one artifact showing meaningful revision after feedback;
one example where your confidence decreased after verification;
one example where AI improved your capability;
one example where AI increased verification cost;
a short transfer reflection.

This is a lab-based course
Although DSA261 is formally a three-hour theoretical course, teaching takes place in the computer laboratory.
You will:

search and trace information;
compare algorithmic environments;
inspect AI claims;
prompt and probe language models;
test context and failure modes;
verify claims against external evidence;
evaluate algorithmic decisions;
document AI-assisted work;
make and defend human judgments.

The goal is not passive familiarity with AI terminology.
The goal is observable capability.

The semester story
The course develops through four connected phases.

Phase I — Know, Search, Question
Weeks 1–3
How do you know whether information is trustworthy?
Who shapes what you see?
What does an AI claim actually mean?
You will work with:

source tracing;
ranking and recommendation;
computational propaganda;
targeting and exposure;
AI claim auditing.




Phase II — Understand and Stress-Test AI
Weeks 4–6
What does an LLM actually do?
What can current AI systems genuinely accomplish?
What happens when algorithms make decisions about people?
You will work with:

language-model mechanics;
context and generation;
capability boundaries;
skill boosting;
verification cost;
bias and fairness;
counterfactual probing.




Week 7 — TRACE Midterm Challenge
The first half of the course comes together in one reconstructable investigation.
The Midterm Challenge asks you to demonstrate:

provenance;
meaningful probing;
evidence quality;
independent verification;
disciplined inference;
transparent AI use;
human judgment.

Open Week 7 — TRACE Midterm Challenge



Phase III — Use, Control, Verify
Weeks 8–11
How do you use AI deliberately rather than merely interact with it?
You will work with:

prompt design;
context management;
context drift;
hallucination;
citation verification;
academic integrity;
AI-use boundaries.




Phase IV — Govern and Transfer
Weeks 12–14
How should AI use change when risk, confidentiality, professional responsibility, or affected people change?
You will work with:

context-specific AI guidelines;
professional accountability;
human oversight;
automation bias;
decision rights;
stop conditions;
transfer to unfamiliar problems.

What you must be able to show
For assessed TRACE work, you may be asked to document:

the prompt or query you used;
how the prompt/query changed;
what the AI contributed;
what you rejected or corrected;
what external evidence you used;
what decision remained yours.

Required evidence for each task
For A, B, and C, submit:

authentic purpose;
task definition;
why AI was or was not appropriate;
model/tool used;
important prompts/interactions;
context supplied;
one meaningful TRACE probe;
independent verification evidence;
one error, failure, limitation, or rejected AI contribution;
final human judgment;
verification cost;
AI-use disclosure.

Cross-task comparison
Compare A, B, and C using common criteria.
At minimum discuss:

where AI increased capability;
where AI increased verification burden;
where prompting mattered;
where context mattered;
where model output was unreliable;
where human judgment could not be delegated;
what transferred across all three tasks;
what remained context-specific.

Genuine failure requirement
Your project must preserve at least one genuine failure case.
Do not hide or replace it with a cleaner example.
Explain:

what failed;
how you detected it;
what risk it created;
how the workflow changed afterward.

Personal AI/LLM Use Charter
Conclude with a one-page charter.
Your charter should state your current rules for:

when to use AI;
when to modify AI output;
when to disclose AI use;
when to reject AI output;
when to verify independently;
when to reset context;
when to stop or escalate;
what responsibility always remains yours.

The charter should emerge from your course evidence, not from generic ethical slogans.

Submission package
Submit:

Task A evidence;
Task B evidence;
Task C evidence;
cross-task comparison;
genuine failure case;
Personal AI/LLM Use Charter;
complete AI-use disclosure;
evidence packet link if required.

Submit the Final Project

Week 7 — TRACE Midterm Challenge
The midterm integrates the first six weeks into one reconstructable investigation.

Week 7 — TRACE Midterm Challenge

The assessment focuses on:

provenance;
search and interaction trails;
meaningful probes;
evidence quality;
verification;
inference discipline;
human judgment;
