Final Project
The course concludes with three authentic discipline-specific tasks and a comparative analysis of AI-assisted work.

Open the TRACE Final Project

The Final Project asks you to demonstrate:

effective task specification;
meaningful probing;
context control;
independent verification;
documented failure or limitation;
explicit human judgment;
transfer across different tasks;
a Personal AI/LLM Use Charter.

Learning goals
By the end of this week, you should be able to:

explain the difference between a claim, a source, and evidence;
distinguish the page where information first reaches you from the original source of that information;
design different search queries for the same information need;
evaluate a source by going beyond the page itself rather than relying only on its appearance;
test a claim against at least one independent source;
explain why your confidence changed—or did not change—after verification.

Task 01 — Source Trace
This is your individual TRACE evidence task for Week 1.
Choose one testable digital claim that you personally encountered during the last seven days. Your job is not merely to label the claim “true” or “false.” You must show, in a reconstructable way, how you moved from first impression to a defensible judgment.

Minimum evidence
Your submission must include:

the original claim and URL or screenshot;
a one-sentence statement of the claim in your own words;
your 0–100 initial confidence before research;
three meaningfully different search queries;
the earliest/original source you could identify, or an explanation of why it could not be identified;
at least one independent verification source;
at least one search result you rejected and why;
a Supported / Contradicted / Unsupported / Missing context / Unresolved verdict;
your 0–100 final confidence;
the most important evidence that changed or strengthened your judgment;
a brief AI-use disclosure, if applicable.







ImportantAI rule for Task 01



You may use AI, for example to brainstorm alternative search queries or to help plan your investigation.
However, LLM output is not evidence. Your final verdict must rest on inspectable external evidence. If you use AI, disclose what you used it for and what parts of verification and judgment you performed yourself.


Submit Task 01 — Source Trace


Completion standard
Complete — A reconstructable search trail, independent evidence, and a defensible verdict are present.
Revise — A conclusion is present, but the evidence trail is incomplete or the supposed verification is not independent.
Missing — The submission contains only opinion/LLM output or does not show a verifiable process.

Learning goals
By the end of this week, you should be able to:

explain why AI is an umbrella term rather than one single technology;
distinguish rule-based automation, machine learning, predictive AI, generative AI, and LLM-based systems at a practical level;
separate a system’s technical function from the way it is marketed;
translate anthropomorphic claims such as “understands”, “knows”, or “predicts people” into testable statements;
evaluate whether available evidence supports a claimed AI capability;
avoid both AI hype and the opposite error of assuming all AI claims are meaningless.

Task 03 — AI Claim Audit
Submit:

original AI claim + URL/screenshot;
exact capability being claimed;
system classification;
rewritten testable claim;
relevant performance metric;
evidence supplied by claimant/vendor;
one independent source;
missing evidence;
final judgment;
AI-use disclosure.

Submit Task 03 — AI Claim Audit

Learning goals
By the end of this week, you should be able to:

distinguish a model benchmark from a general claim about “AI capability”;
explain the METR time-horizon construct and its scope;
identify when extrapolation goes beyond benchmark evidence;
compare unaided, naive-AI, structured-AI, and verified-AI task performance;
recognise verification cost as part of AI-assisted work;
explain why AI access and effective AI use are not the same capability.

Task 05 — Personal Skill Boost Test
Choose one authentic, low-risk task from your discipline.
Submit:

task definition;
baseline/human approach;
naive-AI approach;
structured-AI approach;
verified-AI approach;
time or effort estimate for each condition;
quality criteria;
