Task 09 — Context Stress Test
Choose one non-sensitive task that genuinely benefits from context.
Submit:

target task;
compact relevant context;
mixed/expanded context;
one introduced irrelevant or conflicting element;
fresh-session context brief;
output differences;
evidence of context drift or context robustness;
which context strategy you would use in practice;
what your small test cannot establish;
one independently verified output element;
AI-use disclosure.

Submit Task 09 — Context Stress Test

Learning goals
By the end of this week, you should be able to:

explain why academic integrity is not reducible to a simple AI ban;
distinguish levels of AI involvement in assessment;
align AI permission with a learning objective;
decide when AI output should be used, modified, disclosed, or rejected;
identify when AI use undermines the validity of an assessment;
write a precise AI-use boundary for an assignment.

Before class

REQUIRED READ — AI Assessment Scale
AI Assessment Scale (AIAS)
Explore the current framework
Focus on the five levels:

No AI;
AI Planning;
AI Collaboration;
Full AI;
AI Exploration.

The scale is not a ranking from “bad” to “good”.
The appropriate level depends on the assessment purpose and learning outcomes.


REQUIRED COURSE READING — DSA261 AI Use Policy
Open the course AI Use Policy
Pay particular attention to:

process evidence;
independent verification;
disclosure;
responsibility.



Complete the pre-class quiz
Start Week 11 Pre-Class Quiz

Session 2 — Use, Modify, Disclose, Reject
For an AI contribution, choose one action:

USE
The contribution is appropriate for the task and has been checked to the required standard.


MODIFY
The contribution is useful but incomplete, inaccurate, poorly fitted, or insufficiently yours.


DISCLOSE
The contribution materially affected the work and must be made visible according to the assignment policy.


REJECT
The contribution is unreliable, prohibited, unnecessary, or would undermine the learning objective.
These actions can overlap.
Example:

You may modify + disclose an AI-assisted draft.



Pair Challenge — Boundary cases
Evaluate cases such as:

grammar correction;
idea generation;
outline generation;
source discovery;
summarisation;
first-draft writing;
code generation;
data interpretation;
citation generation.

The correct judgment depends on the assignment, stakes, and required evidence.

Task 11 — AI-Use Boundary Audit
Choose three different academic or professional tasks.
For each task:

state the learning or professional objective;
assign an AIAS level;
define allowed AI use;
define prohibited or inappropriate AI use;
state what must be independently verified;
state what must be disclosed;
apply Use / Modify / Disclose / Reject to one concrete AI contribution;
explain why the boundary fits the task.

Finish with:

one principle that should transfer across all three contexts;
one principle that must remain context-specific;
AI-use disclosure.

Submit Task 11 — AI-Use Boundary Audit

Learning goals
By the end of this week, you should be able to:

distinguish nominal human presence from meaningful human oversight;
explain automation bias;
identify who has authority to accept, reject, override, or stop an AI-assisted process;
define stop conditions before a high-stakes failure occurs;
map accountability across a human–AI workflow;
freeze a Final Project design that specifies responsibility and verification in advance.

Session 1 — Human in the loop is not enough

Today’s Case
A professional receives an AI recommendation and clicks Approve.
Is this meaningful oversight?


Predict
What must the human know or be able to do before “approval” becomes meaningful?


Encounter — Oversight capabilities
A meaningful overseer may need to:

understand the system’s purpose;
understand relevant limitations;
recognise anomalous output;
interpret the recommendation;
seek additional evidence;
reject the output;
override the output;
stop the process;
document the decision.



Probe — Remove authority
Imagine the human can see the output but:

cannot inspect evidence;
cannot change the decision;
cannot stop the process.

Is the person truly overseeing the system?

